{"name":"Titanium White","hex":"#e4e4e4","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"titanium white","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Titanium dioxide when used as a pigment."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/titanium_white"}],"entries":[{"title":"Titanium white","blocks":[{"text":"Titanium white, also Pigment White 6 or CI 77891, is a family of white pigments composed primarily of titanium dioxide. It is the most widely used white pigment in contemporary artistic applications because of its affordability, low toxicity, and high hiding power. Though the term titanium white most often refers to pigments containing titanium dioxide, it can also describe any white pigment that contains a titanium compound."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Arthur_Dove_Silver_Sun.jpg/330px-Arthur_Dove_Silver_Sun.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Titanium_white"},{"title":"Titanium dioxide","blocks":[{"text":"Titanium dioxide, also known as titanium(IV) oxide or titania, is the inorganic compound derived from titanium with the chemical formula TiO2. When used as a pigment, it is called titanium white, Pigment White 6 (PW6), or CI 77891. It is a white solid that is insoluble in water, although mineral forms can appear black. As a pigment, it has a wide range of applications, including paint, sunscreen, and food coloring. When used as a food coloring, it has E number E171. World production in 2014 exceeded 9 million tonnes. It has been estimated that titanium dioxide is used in two-thirds of all pigments, and pigments based on the oxide have been valued at a price of $13.2 billion."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Rutile-unit-cell-3D-balls.png/330px-Rutile-unit-cell-3D-balls.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Titanium_dioxide"},{"title":"Titanium","blocks":[{"text":"Titanium is a chemical element; it has symbol Ti and atomic number 22. Found in nature only as an oxide, it can be reduced to produce a lustrous transition metal with a silver color, low density, and high strength that is resistant to corrosion in sea water, aqua regia, and chlorine."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Titan-crystal_bar.JPG/330px-Titan-crystal_bar.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Titanium"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Titanium%28IV%29_oxide.jpg/500px-Titanium%28IV%29_oxide.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Titanium%28IV%29_oxide.jpg/500px-Titanium%28IV%29_oxide.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The unit cell of rutile","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATitanium(IV)_oxide.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Anatase_crystal_structure.png/330px-Anatase_crystal_structure.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Anatase_crystal_structure.png/330px-Anatase_crystal_structure.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Structure of anatase. Together with rutile and brookite, one of the three major polymorphs of TiO2.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAnatase_crystal_structure.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Industrial_key_players_in_the_production_of_titanium_dioxide.png/500px-Industrial_key_players_in_the_production_of_titanium_dioxide.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Industrial_key_players_in_the_production_of_titanium_dioxide.png/500px-Industrial_key_players_in_the_production_of_titanium_dioxide.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Industrial key players in the production of titanium dioxide - 2022","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIndustrial_key_players_in_the_production_of_titanium_dioxide.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Ferric_sulphate%2C_Kemira.jpg/500px-Ferric_sulphate%2C_Kemira.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Ferric_sulphate%2C_Kemira.jpg/1280px-Ferric_sulphate%2C_Kemira.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Ferric_sulphate%2C_Kemira.jpg/500px-Ferric_sulphate%2C_Kemira.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Residues from sulfate process outside a titanium dioxide production facility in Kaanaa, Pori, Finland.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFerric_sulphate%2C_Kemira.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Relevant_patent_families_describing_titanium_dioxide_production_from_